 ## 2026-08-26
 
+### 0958 — Close Executable Identity Mirror Witnesses
+- ROOT CAUSE: Four production `Dev != … || Ino != …` gates had wrong-device witnesses but no same-device/wrong-inode witness; three inode-removal mutants survived and the force-stop gate was killed only incidentally by a foreign-binary test.
+- FIX: Named production-entry witnesses now cover broker admission, client attestation, broker-candidate scanning, and force-stop before signal; client, broker, and launcher exact-version gates also refuse the below-current direction.
+- FIX: Launcher positive controls consume a target-emitted stdout event instead of a 15s marker-poll deadline; ten repeated controls and the loaded full package passed.
+- EVIDENCE: Seven independent narrowing mutants exited 1 on their named witnesses while exact-version controls stayed green. Focused (`45.328s`), focused race (`67.989s`), full package split (`163.570s`, `2.285s`, `333.802s`), build, vet, gofmt, and diff checks exited 0.
+- SCOPE: `TASK-260826-12laby`; test harness and evidence only, no shared-runtime production behavior changed.
+
 ### 0639 — Bind Force Stop To Kernel Identity Narrowings
 - ROOT CAUSE: Accepted force-stop and status gates compared process start time, argv, UID, and executable device/inode at the production boundary, but their evidence only attacked forged owner records. Kernel-observed UID and same-inode/wrong-device witnesses could not be expressed through record inputs, so narrowing those comparisons left the suite green.
 - FINDING: A literal test-only witness is impossible for those two classes: the record's UID is not a gate input, and one executable identity cannot naturally have the same inode on a different device. A per-call helper is the minimum honest seam; a mutable package global would add a race surface.
